## Test Smell Audit — `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`

**Framework:** MSTest v4 · **Target:** .NET 10 · **Catalog:** testsmells.org (19 smells)

---

### Summary

| Severity | Count |
|----------|-------|
| Critical | 1 |
| High | 2 |
| Medium | 1 |
| Low | 0 |

Overall the suite is **reasonably well-structured**: proper `sealed` class, per-test isolation via in-memory SQLite, clear AAA separation, good naming convention, and meaningful assertions in most tests. Four findings warrant attention.

---

### Critical

#### 1. Empty Test (a.k.a. *Assertion-Free Test*)

**Formal name:** `Empty Test` (testsmells.org)  
**Location:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` — line 113  
**Problem:** Exercises code but asserts nothing. Passes regardless of correctness — gives false confidence.

```csharp
// ❌ Before
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
}

// ✅ After — verify the observable outcome
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

---

### High

#### 2. Sleepy Test

**Formal name:** `Sleepy Test` (testsmells.org)  
**Location:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` — line 87  
**Problem:** `Thread.Sleep(3000)` introduces non-deterministic timing. Even in integration tests, a hard 3-second wall-clock sleep is a flakiness vector (CI load, slower hardware).

```csharp
// ❌ Before
Thread.Sleep(3000);
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));

// ✅ After — poll with timeout (or use an event/semaphore from the SUT)
var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
{
    await Task.Delay(100, cts.Token);
}
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Integration-test calibration:** A short sleep (<500 ms) for an *inherently asynchronous external system* can be tolerable; 3 seconds blocking the thread is excessive even for integration work.

---

#### 3. Conditional Test Logic

**Formal name:** `Conditional Test Logic` (testsmells.org)  
**Location:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` — line 101  
**Problem:** `if/else` branching means only one path is verified per run. The test can silently take the "wrong" branch and still pass, hiding a bug.

```csharp
// ❌ Before — assertion depends on runtime type
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
else
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);

// ✅ After — split into two explicit tests
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsName()
{
    // arrange a known standard user…
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasDiscount()
{
    // arrange a known premium user…
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

---

### Medium

#### 4. Assertion Roulette

**Formal name:** `Assertion Roulette` (testsmells.org)  
**Location:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` — lines 35-38 (4 asserts, no messages)  
**Problem:** If any of the 4 assertions fail, the failure message doesn't immediately distinguish *which* business expectation was violated. In MSTest, adding a message parameter aids diagnosis in CI logs.

**Integration-test calibration:** Multiple assertions on a *single logical outcome* (one inserted record) are reasonable. Downgraded from High to Medium because the assertions are sequential and the default failure output includes expected/actual values. Still worth adding messages for CI clarity.

---

### Positive Observations

| Practice | Detail |
|----------|--------|
| ✅ Per-test isolation | Fresh in-memory SQLite per test via `TestInitialize`/`TestCleanup` — no ordering dependency |
| ✅ Proper resource cleanup | `_connection.Dispose()` in `[TestCleanup]` |
| ✅ `sealed` test class | Prevents accidental inheritance coupling |
| ✅ Good naming convention | `Method_Scenario_ExpectedResult` consistently |
| ✅ `[TestCategory("Integration")]` | Proper tagging for selective execution |
| ✅ Real assertions in most tests | Strong behavioral verification (not just null checks) |
| ✅ Concurrency test | `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` is a well-written integration concern |

---

### Fix Priority

| Priority | Smell | Effort |
|----------|-------|--------|
| 1 | Empty Test (`BulkInsert`) | 2 min — add one assertion |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic (`GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType`) | 10 min — split into 2 tests |
| 3 | Sleepy Test (`NotifyOnInsert`) | 15 min — replace with poll loop |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette (`InsertUser`) | 5 min — add message params |

**Verdict:** The suite is solid for integration tests. Fix the one Critical (assertion-free test) immediately; address the two High items in your next sprint.
